WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.172
Welcome to Module 9, Data Cleaning in Postgres.

00:00:04.172 --> 00:00:08.762
Before we start, think about how much SQL you can already use.

00:00:08.762 --> 00:00:16.972
In the earlier modules, you learned how relational databases organize data, how to query tables with S Q L,

00:00:16.972 --> 00:00:24.862
how to aggregate values, apply logical conditions, join related tables, and use subqueries.

00:00:24.862 --> 00:00:32.722
In the previous module, you also worked with strings and dates, including functions that help us transform text and

00:00:32.722 --> 00:00:34.433
handle date values.

00:00:35.178 --> 00:00:42.930
Now we will bring many of those skills together for a very practical problem: real data is rarely clean.

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:46.290
A customer name may contain extra spaces.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:49.935
An email may use inconsistent capitalization.

00:00:49.935 --> 00:00:54.025
A phone number may contain parentheses, spaces, or dashes.

00:00:54.025 --> 00:00:58.250
Some values may be missing, invalid, or duplicated.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:02.080
The goal is not simply to make the data look nicer.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:09.865
Our goal is to profile the data, preserve the original evidence, standardize values when we understand the rule,

00:01:09.865 --> 00:01:13.590
validate what we can, identify duplicates, and make changes safely.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:22.220
By the end of this module, I want you to think of data cleaning as a careful and explainable process rather than a

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.695
collection of quick fixes.
